The reduction in communication was largest for the heterogeneous configurations, where depleted nodes could defer their transmissions to better-provisioned neighbours. In homogeneous configurations the protocol behaved like the baseline, which is the expected outcome given that every node faces the same trade-off between information gain and energy expenditure.

\section{Discussion}
The results indicate that energy heterogeneity, often treated as a nuisance, can be exploited to reduce the overall communication load. Nevertheless several limitations remain. The protocol assumes that energy levels can be estimated accurately, which may not hold for nodes with ageing batteries. Moreover, adversarial nodes could report misleading energy levels to avoid transmitting, a concern that we leave for future work.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented an adaptive consensus protocol for heterogeneous sensor networks that exploits differences in energy budgets to reduce communication overhead. Theoretical analysis and a field deployment demonstrate that the protocol preserves convergence guarantees while transmitting forty percent fewer messages than established approaches. Future work will address adversarial participants and mobility.

